Precise sleeps: OpenBSD's clock ticks at 100 Hz (Sleep(1) takes 20 ms).
When the process asked for a fine timer resolution (NtSetTimerResolution,
via timeBeginPeriod) or WINE_PRECISE_SLEEP=1 is set, NtDelayExecution
sleeps in the kernel only while more than two ticks remain and yields for
the rest.
Timed waits on objects end on a tick in the server too; in the same mode
NtWaitForSingleObject, NtWaitForMultipleObjects and alertable
NtDelayExecution wait in the server while more than two ticks remain and
then poll the objects every 100 us.

